{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>You shall speak, saying.</b> One needs to know why Aaron should speak here to Israel and not Moses, as is usual? Perhaps it is because he had said to them, ״Whoever has gold, let them break it off״ and so forth (Exodus 32:24) — therefore HaShem commanded him that he should speak to them to bring a goat as atonement: the mouth that bound is the mouth that unbound. And we have already written in several places the words of our Sages of blessed memory, that sin activates the blemish in its place, and the rectification must be directed against it. And that is what the verse says, <b>you shall speak, saying</b> — meaning, you shall speak a speech that contains merit and virtue corresponding to the speech in which there was the degradation with which he loosened the people." "Further, it appears that since Aaron had ascended to this wondrous eminence — from among all Israel did HaShem choose him — therefore Moses said to him that he should speak gentle words to Israel, and that is what the verse says, <b>saying</b>. And so you will find that it says, <b>for today HaShem appears to you</b> — meaning, all that is attained and all the desire is only for the sake of Israel, as it says, ״to you״ and not ״to me.״ And in the manner that we find Moses said to Israel: is it not so that as long as Israel was rebuked, HaShem did not speak with Moses — for all that is attained is only by virtue of the merit of Israel."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:54:31.443985132Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}